==============================================================================
PART B -- the tournament-on-6 bijection test
==============================================================================
A000568(6) = 56 = # iso classes of tournaments on 6 vertices.
  brute tournament iso classes n=3: 2  (A000568=2) OK
  brute tournament iso classes n=4: 4  (A000568=4) OK
  brute tournament iso classes n=5: 12  (A000568=12) OK

Candidate bijection 1: '6 elements, support-3 structure = a tournament'.
  For a 6-set E, orient each pair {a,b} by an additive rule and ask whether
  the resulting structures, up to iso, number 56.
  PROBLEM (honest): a tournament needs a DEFINED orientation on ALL C(6,2)=15
  pairs, but support-3 relations only touch SOME triples -- E's support-3
  hypergraph is NOT a tournament (it is a 3-uniform partial hypergraph).
  So a literal 'shape <-> tournament' map cannot be a bijection by type alone.

  We therefore test the WEAKER claim: does SOME natural support-3 count = 56?
  6-sets in [0,10], all 6 vertices in support-3 edges: 372 distinct shapes
  6-sets in [0,12], all 6 vertices in support-3 edges: 1165 distinct shapes

Candidate bijection 2: the SINGLE-TRIANGLE challenger.  A challenger shape with
exactly ONE support-3 hyperedge has only finitely many ROLE TYPES:
  AP triple: 1 way to label (middle distinguished, 2 ends symmetric) ;
  Schur triple: 1 way (sum distinguished, 2 addends symmetric).
=> only 2 single-triangle shapes.  Not 56.  The '56' is NOT a single triple.

Candidate bijection 3: the support-3 COEFFICIENT SIGNATURES with |coef|<=3.
Enumerate primitive (p,q,r), gcd=1, canonical sign, that can vanish on 3
DISTINCT positive reals -- count them by |coef| bound:
  |coef|<= 2: 21 ordered signatures, 10 unordered  
  |coef|<= 3: 75 ordered signatures, 32 unordered  
  |coef|<= 4: 165 ordered signatures, 66 unordered  

